"""
NCERT Class 5 Mathematics - Chapter 5: Far and Near Data
"""

slides_data = [{'id': 1, 'heading': 'Choosing Units', 'tagline': 'Selecting the Appropriate Unit: Millimetres, Centimetres, Metres, or Kilometres', 'color': 'from-amber-500 to-orange-600', 'badge': 'Class 5 Maths • Chapter 5', 'audio_text': 'Welcome, young explorers and mathematicians! Today we journey into the world of spatial distance with Far and Near. In our daily lives, we measure lengths of all kinds: from the tiny thickness of an eraser to the towering height of monument gates and the vast highway distances between cities. To measure effectively, our very first task is to choose the most sensible unit of measurement! If you measure a small object like a pencil, a handkerchief, or the width of your notebook, centimetres provide the ideal scale. If you measure a taller structure like the historic India Gate in New Delhi, the depth of a village drinking well, or the length of a cricket pitch, metres are the natural choice! Using centimetres to describe a highway between Delhi and Jaipur would require hundreds of thousands of tiny numbers, which is clumsy and confusing. Instead, we use kilometres for long travel distances. By choosing the appropriate metric unit, we make measurements simple, readable, and practical.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'The Metric Hierarchy', 'content': 'Millimetre (mm) for tiny thicknesses; Centimetre (cm) for hand-held objects; Metre (m) for rooms, sports pitches, and monuments; Kilometre (km) for road and rail travel.', 'icon': '📏'}, {'title': 'Real-World Matching', 'content': 'Handkerchief = ~25 cm; India Gate Height = ~42 m; Well Depth = ~15 m; Highway between cities = ~250 km.', 'icon': '🏛️'}, {'title': 'Sensible Units Principle', 'content': 'Choose the unit whose numerical value is easy to read, write, and communicate without excessively huge or awkward fractions.', 'icon': '🎯'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Which unit is best suited to measure the height of the historic India Gate in New Delhi?', 'options': ['Metres (m)', 'Centimetres (cm)', 'Millimetres (mm)', 'Kilometres (km)'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! India Gate stands approximately 42 metres tall, making metres the most practical unit.'}, {'question': 'Which unit should you use to measure the length of a cotton handkerchief?', 'options': ['Centimetres (cm)', 'Kilometres (km)', 'Metres (m)', 'Miles'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! A handkerchief is around 25 to 30 centimetres long, ideal for a standard ruler.'}]}, {'id': 2, 'heading': 'Unit Equivalence', 'tagline': 'Connecting Centimetres and Metres Using Double Number Lines', 'color': 'from-blue-600 to-indigo-700', 'badge': 'Equivalence on Double Lines', 'audio_text': 'Lengths can be expressed in different units while representing the exact same physical distance! The golden conversion bridge between centimetres and metres is: 100 centimetres equals 1 metre! Therefore, 200 centimetres equals 2 metres, 300 centimetres equals 3 metres, and 500 centimetres equals 5 metres. What if a fabric stole or sari measures 240 centimetres? Let us partition 240 centimetres into hundreds and remaining tens: 240 is 200 centimetres plus 40 centimetres. Since 200 centimetres equals 2 metres, 240 centimetres is exactly 2 metres and 40 centimetres! Our textbook visualizes this relationship using double number lines: a top scale graduated in metres and a bottom scale graduated in centimetres. Every mark on the metre line aligns perfectly with its hundredfold value on the centimetre line below it! Double number lines allow you to translate seamlessly between single-unit and mixed-unit descriptions.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'The Golden Rule: 1 m = 100 cm', 'content': 'To convert metres to centimetres, multiply by 100. To convert centimetres to metres, divide by 100 (or separate into hundreds).', 'icon': '⚖️'}, {'title': 'Double Number Line Alignment', 'content': '0 m = 0 cm; 1 m = 100 cm; 2 m = 200 cm; 2.4 m = 240 cm; 5 m = 500 cm. Dual scales make equivalence visually intuitive.', 'icon': '📊'}, {'title': 'Sari & Fabric Measures', 'content': 'Traditional Indian saris measure 550 cm (5 m 50 cm), and dupattas or stoles measure 240 cm (2 m 40 cm).', 'icon': '🧵'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'How is 240 centimetres expressed in mixed metres and centimetres?', 'options': ['2 metres 40 centimetres', '24 metres', '20 metres 4 centimetres', '2 metres 4 centimetres'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! 240 cm = 200 cm + 40 cm = 2 m 40 cm.'}, {'question': 'A silk sari is 5 metres and 50 centimetres long. What is its length in centimetres alone?', 'options': ['550 cm', '5,500 cm', '505 cm', '55 cm'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! 5 m = 500 cm, so 500 cm + 50 cm = 550 cm.'}]}, {'id': 3, 'heading': 'Comparing Lengths', 'tagline': 'Standardizing to a Single Unit Before Applying Inequalities', 'color': 'from-teal-600 to-cyan-700', 'badge': 'Comparative Analysis', 'audio_text': 'When comparing two lengths expressed in different units, we must never compare the numbers blindly! For example, if you compare 456 centimetres with 5 metres, you might be tempted to think 456 is larger than 5 because 456 is a bigger number. However, you must remember that each metre contains 100 centimetres! To compare correctly, first convert both quantities into the exact same common unit. Let us convert 5 metres into centimetres: 5 times 100 gives 500 centimetres! Now compare: 456 centimetres is strictly less than 500 centimetres. Therefore, 456 centimetres is less than 5 metres! We write: 456 cm is less than 5 m, using the opening-to-the-larger-value inequality symbol. Always equalize units before comparing, ordering, adding, or subtracting lengths.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'The Golden Rule of Comparison', 'content': 'Never compare raw numbers of different units! Always convert all quantities into the same unit (usually the smaller unit, such as cm) first.', 'icon': '🔍'}, {'title': 'Inequality Signs (<, =, >)', 'content': 'The wide open mouth always faces the greater value: 456 cm < 500 cm (5 m); 300 cm = 3 m; 6 m 20 cm > 600 cm.', 'icon': '⚖️'}, {'title': 'Bridge Calculation', 'content': 'To compare 3 m 25 cm with 350 cm: 3 m 25 cm = 325 cm. Since 325 < 350, 3 m 25 cm < 350 cm.', 'icon': '🌉'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Which symbol correctly completes the comparison: 456 cm ___ 5 m?', 'options': ['< (Less than)', '> (Greater than)', '= (Equal to)', '≈ (Approximately)'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! 5 m = 500 cm. Since 456 cm < 500 cm, 456 cm < 5 m.'}, {'question': 'Which length is the greatest: 2 m 80 cm, 300 cm, or 295 cm?', 'options': ['300 cm (3 m)', '2 m 80 cm (280 cm)', '295 cm', 'They are all equal'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! Converting to cm: 280 cm, 300 cm, 295 cm. 300 cm is the greatest.'}]}, {'id': 4, 'heading': 'Tallest Statues', 'tagline': 'Height Data and Proportional Analysis of World Monuments', 'color': 'from-emerald-600 to-green-700', 'badge': 'Architectural Heights', 'audio_text': 'Our textbook features an inspiring architectural study: the tallest statues in the world! Standing proudly on the banks of the Narmada River in Gujarat, India, is the magnificent Statue of Unity, depicting Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel. It is the tallest statue in the world, soaring to an astonishing height of 182 metres! Notice how significant this number is: 182 matches the total number of constituencies in the Gujarat Legislative Assembly. The second tallest statue is the Spring Temple Buddha in China, standing at 128 metres. And the famous Statue of Liberty in New York, USA, stands at 93 metres including its pedestal. Let us compare their heights using subtraction! How much taller is the Statue of Unity than the Spring Temple Buddha? We subtract: 182 metres minus 128 metres equals 54 metres! And how does the Statue of Unity compare to the Statue of Liberty? 182 minus 93 equals 89 metres taller: nearly double the height of the Statue of Liberty! Height comparisons allow us to appreciate the grand scale of monumental engineering.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'Statue of Unity (India)', 'content': 'Height: 182 metres (597 feet). Located in Kevadia (Ekta Nagar), Gujarat. The tallest statue on Earth, built to withstand earthquakes and high wind speeds.', 'icon': '🇮🇳'}, {'title': 'Spring Temple Buddha (China)', 'content': 'Height: 128 metres (420 feet). The second tallest statue in the world, surpassed by the Statue of Unity by 54 metres.', 'icon': '🏯'}, {'title': 'Statue of Liberty (USA)', 'content': 'Height: 93 metres (305 feet) from ground to torch tip. The Statue of Unity is nearly twice as tall (182 m vs 93 m).', 'icon': '🗽'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': "What is the height of India's Statue of Unity?", 'options': ['182 metres', '128 metres', '93 metres', '200 metres'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! The Statue of Unity stands at 182 metres tall.'}, {'question': 'How much taller is the Statue of Unity (182 m) than the Spring Temple Buddha (128 m)?', 'options': ['54 metres', '64 metres', '44 metres', '50 metres'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! 182 - 128 = 54 metres.'}]}, {'id': 5, 'heading': 'Measuring Tools', 'tagline': 'Instruments for Measuring Long Distances on Earth', 'color': 'from-purple-600 to-indigo-700', 'badge': 'Surveying Instruments', 'audio_text': "How do engineers, surveyors, and sports officials measure long outdoor distances accurately? A standard 30-centimetre ruler is wonderful for school notebooks, but measuring a whole football field or a newly paved road with a small ruler would take forever! Instead, specialized long-distance instruments are employed: First, the long surveyor tape: sturdy flexible tapes made of fiberglass or steel that extend 30, 50, or 100 metres! Second, the rodometer, commonly called a measuring wheel: as a surveyor pushes this single wheel along the ground, a mechanical or digital counter tracks each revolution, measuring the road distance smoothly in metres. Third, the laser distance meter: an electronic device that emits a safe laser beam to a distant wall and calculates the exact distance in fractions of a second based on the light's reflection time! And fourth, optical rangefinders used by sports coaches and navigators. Each instrument is designed for precision across different terrains.", 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'Measuring Wheel (Rodometer)', 'content': 'A rolling wheel with a calibrated circumference (often exactly 1 metre). Each full rotation records 1 metre on the attached counter.', 'icon': '🎡'}, {'title': 'Laser Distance Meter', 'content': 'Uses laser reflection to measure distances up to 200 metres with millimeter precision in a fraction of a second.', 'icon': '🔦'}, {'title': 'Surveyor Long Tape', 'content': 'Reinforced steel or fiberglass ribbon tape extending 30 m, 50 m, or 100 m, used on athletics tracks and construction foundations.', 'icon': '📏'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Which tool is commonly rolled along the road by highway surveyors to measure long road lengths?', 'options': ['A measuring wheel (rodometer)', 'A 15 cm school ruler', 'A sewing needle', 'A compass divider'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! A measuring wheel (rodometer) records revolutions as it rolls, measuring long distances conveniently.'}, {'question': 'How does a laser distance meter calculate distance?', 'options': ['By measuring the time taken for a laser beam to reflect back from a target', 'By rolling along the ground', 'By weighing the air between two points', 'By counting the footsteps of the operator'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! It uses the speed of light and the reflection time to calculate distance with extreme accuracy.'}]}, {'id': 6, 'heading': 'The Kilometre', 'tagline': 'Unveiling the Kilometre: 1,000 Metres of Ground Distance', 'color': 'from-rose-600 to-pink-700', 'badge': '1 km = 1,000 m', 'audio_text': "Now let us step outside onto the school playground to discover the kilometre! The prefix 'kilo' comes from an ancient Greek word meaning one thousand. Therefore, 1 kilometre represents exactly 1,000 metres! How far is 1,000 metres in human terms? If your school athletics running track is 200 metres long, you would need to run around it five complete times to complete 1 kilometre: 5 times 200 equals 1,000 metres! If an average Grade 5 student's walking pace spans approximately 50 centimetres (half a metre), it takes about 2,000 footsteps to walk one single kilometre! Walking a kilometre at a brisk pace takes about 12 to 15 minutes. When we travel between towns and states, highway milestones show distances in kilometres because metres would be too cumbersome to write on road signs. Always remember: 1 kilometre equals 1,000 metres, and half a kilometre equals 500 metres!", 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'The 1 km = 1,000 m Rule', 'content': "'Kilo' means 1,000. 1 km = 1,000 m; 1/2 km = 500 m; 1/4 km = 250 m; 3/4 km = 750 m.", 'icon': '🛣️'}, {'title': 'Footsteps & Time', 'content': 'Average step = ~50 cm (0.5 m). 1 km = 2,000 footsteps. Brisk walking time for 1 km = 12 to 15 minutes.', 'icon': '👟'}, {'title': 'Highway Milestones', 'content': 'Yellow and white stones mark National Highways; green and white mark State Highways, showing distances in kilometres.', 'icon': '🪨'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'How many metres are there in 1 kilometre?', 'options': ['1,000 metres', '100 metres', '10 metres', '10,000 metres'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': "Correct! 'Kilo' means thousand, so 1 km = 1,000 m."}, {'question': 'If a running track is 250 metres long, how many full laps equal 1 kilometre?', 'options': ['4 laps (4 × 250 = 1,000 m)', '2 laps', '5 laps', '10 laps'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! 4 laps × 250 m = 1,000 m = 1 km.'}]}, {'id': 7, 'heading': 'Race Track', 'tagline': 'Planning Logistics for a 3-Kilometre School Marathon', 'color': 'from-amber-600 to-yellow-600', 'badge': 'Event Planning Logistics', 'audio_text': 'Let us apply kilometre arithmetic to organize a community 3-kilometre mini-marathon from our textbook! A 3-kilometre race covers a total distance of: 3 times 1,000 metres, which is 3,000 metres! To keep runners hydrated and safe, race organizers place water stations, medical tents, and directional flag marshals at equal intervals along the course. Suppose the organizers decide to place a water station every 500 metres starting after the start line. How many water stations will there be? Let us calculate the points along the course: Station 1 at 500 metres; Station 2 at 1,000 metres (which is 1 km); Station 3 at 1,500 metres (1.5 km); Station 4 at 2,000 metres (2 km); Station 5 at 2,500 metres (2.5 km); and the final celebration at the 3,000-metre finish line! That gives five intermediate refreshment stations along the course. Breaking long race distances into metric intervals ensures perfect event logistics.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': '3 km in Metres', 'content': '3 km = 3 × 1,000 m = 3,000 metres. A 5-km race = 5,000 m; a 10-km race = 10,000 m.', 'icon': '🏃\u200d♂️'}, {'title': 'Water Station Intervals', 'content': 'Dividing total distance by interval length: 3,000 m / 500 m = 6 segments, giving 5 intermediate refreshment stations.', 'icon': '💧'}, {'title': 'Safety & Marshal Points', 'content': 'Marshals placed every 250 m ensure complete visual safety and correct navigation around race turns.', 'icon': '🚩'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'How many metres are in a 3-kilometre race course?', 'options': ['3,000 metres', '300 metres', '30,000 metres', '1,500 metres'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! 3 km = 3 × 1,000 m = 3,000 m.'}, {'question': 'If a water station is placed every 500 metres, at what distance mark is the second water station?', 'options': ['1,000 metres (1 km)', '500 metres', '1,500 metres', '2,000 metres'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! Station 1 is at 500 m, Station 2 is at 500 + 500 = 1,000 m (1 km).'}]}, {'id': 8, 'heading': 'Train Journey', 'tagline': 'The Vivek Express: 4,189 Kilometres Across 9 Indian States', 'color': 'from-blue-600 to-indigo-700', 'badge': 'Rail Route Arithmetic', 'audio_text': 'Did you know that the longest train journey in India is operated by the historic Vivek Express? Introduced to commemorate the 150th birth anniversary of Swami Vivekananda, this extraordinary train runs all the way from Dibrugarh in Assam in the northeast, down to Kanniyakumari in Tamil Nadu at the southernmost tip of peninsular India! The total route covers a staggering distance of 4,189 kilometres! The train passes through 9 Indian states and takes around 75 hours to complete the journey, making over 55 major scheduled stops. Let us examine intermediate station distances: From Dibrugarh to Guwahati is approximately 560 kilometres. From Guwahati onward to Malda Town in West Bengal is about 640 kilometres. By calculating distances between consecutive railway junctions, railway dispatchers create timetable schedules, plan locomotive refuelling, and ensure passenger safety across thousands of kilometres! Indian Railways unites our diverse nation through precision mathematics.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'Vivek Express Record', 'content': 'Route: Dibrugarh (Assam) to Kanniyakumari (Tamil Nadu). Distance: 4,189 km. Duration: ~75 hours. 9 Indian states traversed.', 'icon': '🚆'}, {'title': 'Station Intervals', 'content': 'Distance between stations = Milestone(Station B) - Milestone(Station A). Analyzing route segments teaches multi-digit subtraction.', 'icon': '🚉'}, {'title': 'Time and Speed', 'content': 'Average speed = Total Distance / Total Time. 4,189 km / 75 hours ≈ 56 km/h (including stops across varied terrain).', 'icon': '⏱️'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': "What is the total route length of the Vivek Express, India's longest train journey?", 'options': ['4,189 kilometres', '1,820 kilometres', '2,500 kilometres', '6,000 kilometres'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! The Vivek Express travels 4,189 km from Dibrugarh to Kanniyakumari.'}, {'question': 'If a train travels 560 km in Segment 1 and 640 km in Segment 2, what is the combined distance of both segments?', 'options': ['1,200 km', '1,100 km', '1,300 km', '1,190 km'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! 560 + 640 = 1,200 km.'}]}, {'id': 9, 'heading': 'Millimetre Scale', 'tagline': 'The World of Small Things: 1 Centimetre Divided into 10 Millimetres', 'color': 'from-teal-600 to-emerald-700', 'badge': 'Sub-Centimetre Precision', 'audio_text': 'We have explored kilometres and metres; now let us zoom in to the fascinating world of small things! Take your standard plastic school ruler and look closely at the space between 0 and 1 centimetre. Notice the tiny little tick marks packed in between: there are exactly 10 equal subdivisions! Each of these tiny subdivisions is called one millimetre, abbreviated as mm. Therefore, the golden rule of small measurements is: 1 centimetre equals 10 millimetres! Half a centimetre is the slightly longer middle mark, representing exactly 5 millimetres. Think of objects that require millimetres: the thickness of a coin is about 2 millimetres; the lead tip of a mechanical pencil is often 0.5 or 0.7 millimetres; and a tiny black ant is around 3 to 4 millimetres long. When a line measures 2 centimetres and 6 tiny marks, we write it as 2 centimetres 6 millimetres, or 26 millimetres! Millimetres give us microscope-level precision in our drawings.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'The 1 cm = 10 mm Rule', 'content': 'Every centimetre is partitioned into 10 equal millimetres. To convert cm to mm, multiply by 10 (e.g. 4 cm = 40 mm).', 'icon': '🔬'}, {'title': 'Reading Ruler Subdivisions', 'content': 'Long mark = 1 cm; Mid mark = 5 mm (half-cm); Short ticks = 1 mm each. 3 cm + 4 ticks = 3 cm 4 mm = 34 mm.', 'icon': '📏'}, {'title': 'Everyday Millimetre Objects', 'content': 'Thickness of credit card = ~1 mm; 5-rupee coin = ~2 mm; Grains of rice width = ~2 mm; Mobile phone thickness = ~8 mm.', 'icon': '🪙'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'How many millimetres are there in 1 centimetre?', 'options': ['10 millimetres', '100 millimetres', '5 millimetres', '1,000 millimetres'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! Each centimetre contains exactly 10 equal millimetres.'}, {'question': 'A line measures 4 centimetres and 7 millimetres. What is its total length in millimetres alone?', 'options': ['47 millimetres', '407 millimetres', '4.7 metres', '40 millimetres'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! 4 cm = 40 mm, plus 7 mm = 47 mm.'}]}, {'id': 10, 'heading': 'Seed Growth', 'tagline': 'Tracking Sprouted Moong and Chana Growth Over 4 Days', 'color': 'from-emerald-600 to-teal-700', 'badge': 'Biological Measurement Activity', 'audio_text': "Our textbook invites us to conduct an exciting scientific measuring experiment: observing sprouted seeds! Take a few dried moong or chana seeds, soak them in water overnight, and wrap them in a moist cotton cloth. Within twenty-four hours, life begins to stir, and a tiny white radicle sprout pushes out! Using our ruler's millimetre scale, we record the sprout's growth each morning: On Day 1, the tiny shoot emerges, measuring about 3 millimetres. On Day 2, it extends to 8 millimetres: growing by 5 millimetres in a single day! On Day 3, the vigorous sprout reaches 15 millimetres, which we can also write as 1 centimetre and 5 millimetres. By Day 4, the sprout stretches to 24 millimetres, or 2 centimetres and 4 millimetres! By tracking daily growth with millimetre precision, we combine biology, observational recording, and metric measurement. Notice how rapid growth turns invisible daily increments into visible centimetres.", 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'Sprout Growth Journal', 'content': 'Day 1: 3 mm; Day 2: 8 mm (+5 mm); Day 3: 15 mm = 1 cm 5 mm (+7 mm); Day 4: 24 mm = 2 cm 4 mm (+9 mm).', 'icon': '🌱'}, {'title': 'Growth Rate Calculation', 'content': 'Daily Growth = Length(Day N) - Length(Day N-1). Example: Day 3 Growth = 15 mm - 8 mm = 7 mm.', 'icon': '📈'}, {'title': 'Dual Notation', 'content': 'Lengths can be expressed as single mm (24 mm) or mixed cm and mm (2 cm 4 mm). Both are mathematically equivalent.', 'icon': '📝'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'A sprouted chana seed measures 18 millimetres. How is this expressed in centimetres and millimetres?', 'options': ['1 cm 8 mm', '18 cm', '10 cm 8 mm', '1 cm 80 mm'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! 18 mm = 10 mm + 8 mm = 1 cm 8 mm.'}, {'question': 'A sprout grew from 6 mm on Monday to 14 mm on Tuesday. How much did it grow in one day?', 'options': ['8 mm', '20 mm', '6 mm', '14 mm'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! 14 mm - 6 mm = 8 mm of growth.'}]}, {'id': 11, 'heading': 'Precision Lines', 'tagline': 'Drawing Accurate Line Segments and Conversions on Double Number Lines', 'color': 'from-indigo-600 to-purple-700', 'badge': 'Drafting Precision', 'audio_text': 'In engineering, architecture, and art, drawing lines of exact length is an essential skill! To draw a line segment of 5 centimetres and 5 millimetres: Place your ruler firmly on the paper. Use a sharp pencil point to mark your starting point at exactly 0. Draw along the edge past the 5-centimetre mark, counting 5 small millimetre ticks to the half-centimetre line. Mark the endpoint and lift your pencil. You have created an exact segment of 55 millimetres! What if you are asked to draw a line of 36 millimetres? Think: 36 millimetres is 30 millimetres plus 6 millimetres. Since 30 millimetres equals 3 centimetres, 36 millimetres is exactly 3 centimetres and 6 millimetres! Double number lines help us verify these conversions effortlessly: matching mm to cm, cm to m, and m to km. Precision drafting ensures that parts built in different workshops fit together with flawless accuracy.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'Exact Drawing Steps', 'content': 'Step 1: Anchor at 0 (never at the edge of ruler). Step 2: Draw to the whole cm mark. Step 3: Count small mm ticks. Step 4: Double-check endpoints.', 'icon': '📐'}, {'title': 'Conversion: mm to cm', 'content': 'Divide by 10. Example: 36 mm = 3.6 cm = 3 cm 6 mm. 70 mm = 7 cm. 95 mm = 9 cm 5 mm.', 'icon': '🔄'}, {'title': 'Architectural Drafting', 'content': 'Blueprints use millimetres as the universal standard so that builders never mistake feet for metres or centimetres.', 'icon': '🏛️'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'Where should you align the starting point of a line when measuring with a ruler?', 'options': ['At the 0 mark', 'At the physical edge of the wooden or plastic ruler', 'At the 1 cm mark', 'Anywhere in the middle'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! Always start at the 0 mark, as the physical edge of a ruler may be worn or indented.'}, {'question': 'A line is 75 millimetres long. What is this length in centimetres and millimetres?', 'options': ['7 cm 5 mm', '75 cm', '70 cm 5 mm', '7 cm 50 mm'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! 75 mm = 70 mm + 5 mm = 7 cm 5 mm.'}]}, {'id': 12, 'heading': 'Adding Lengths', 'tagline': 'Column Addition for Mixed Units with Metric Regrouping', 'color': 'from-blue-600 to-indigo-700', 'badge': 'Mixed Unit Addition', 'audio_text': 'When combining ribbons, cables, or highway route legs, we often add mixed units of length! We can add lengths using two reliable methods: Method 1 is column addition: create separate columns for each unit, such as Metres and Centimetres, or Kilometres and Metres. Suppose we add 4 metres 65 centimetres and 3 metres 55 centimetres. Add the centimetres column first: 65 plus 55 equals 120 centimetres! Because 100 centimetres equals 1 metre, 120 centimetres is 1 metre and 20 centimetres. We write 20 in the centimetres column and carry over 1 metre to the metres column! Now add the metres: 4 plus 3 plus the carried 1 gives 8 metres. The total is 8 metres 20 centimetres! Method 2 is conversion: convert everything to centimetres first: 465 cm plus 355 cm equals 820 cm, which equals 8 m 20 cm. Both methods give the exact same answer, giving you complete flexibility.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'Regrouping Thresholds', 'content': 'For cm to m: 100 cm bundles into 1 m (carry over at 100). For m to km: 1,000 m bundles into 1 km (carry over at 1,000).', 'icon': '📦'}, {'title': 'Column Addition Method', 'content': 'Align smaller units on the right. Add smaller units first. If sum >= base, write remainder and carry bundle to the left.', 'icon': '📊'}, {'title': 'Conversion Method', 'content': 'Convert both terms to the smallest unit (e.g. 4 m 65 cm = 465 cm). Add as whole numbers, then separate back into mixed units.', 'icon': '🔄'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is the sum of 5 m 45 cm and 2 m 70 cm?', 'options': ['8 m 15 cm', '7 m 15 cm', '8 m 25 cm', '7 m 115 cm'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! cm: 45 + 70 = 115 cm = 1 m 15 cm (write 15, carry 1 m). Metres: 5 + 2 + 1 = 8 m. Total = 8 m 15 cm.'}, {'question': 'Add 3 km 600 m and 4 km 750 m. What is the total distance?', 'options': ['8 km 350 m', '7 km 350 m', '8 km 450 m', '7 km 1,350 m'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! m: 600 + 750 = 1,350 m = 1 km 350 m. km: 3 + 4 + 1 = 8 km. Total = 8 km 350 m.'}]}, {'id': 13, 'heading': 'Subtracting Lengths', 'tagline': 'Column Subtraction with Borrowing Across Metric Units', 'color': 'from-rose-600 to-red-700', 'badge': 'Mixed Unit Subtraction', 'audio_text': 'Subtracting lengths is essential when calculating remaining distances, leftover fabric, or route differences! Just like standard subtraction, when the top number in the smaller unit is less than the bottom number, we must borrow from the larger unit. Consider this: A tailor has a 6-metre roll of silk ribbon and cuts off 2 metres and 45 centimetres for a dress. How much ribbon remains? Let us subtract: 6 metres 00 centimetres minus 2 metres 45 centimetres. In the centimetres column, 0 minus 45 is not possible! We borrow 1 metre from the 6 metres, leaving 5 metres. Remember: 1 borrowed metre equals 100 centimetres! Now subtract centimetres: 100 minus 45 equals 55 centimetres. Subtract metres: 5 minus 2 equals 3 metres. The remaining ribbon is 3 metres 55 centimetres! Let us check by addition: 2 metres 45 cm plus 3 metres 55 cm equals 6 metres 00 cm. Perfectly verified!', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'Borrowing from Metres', 'content': 'Borrowing 1 metre gives exactly 100 centimetres (not 10!). 0 cm becomes 100 cm; 25 cm becomes 125 cm.', 'icon': '🔄'}, {'title': 'Borrowing from Kilometres', 'content': 'Borrowing 1 kilometre gives exactly 1,000 metres! 200 m becomes 1,200 m.', 'icon': '🛣️'}, {'title': 'Verification Check', 'content': 'Always check: Remaining Length + Cut Length = Original Roll. 3 m 55 cm + 2 m 45 cm = 6 m.', 'icon': '✅'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'What is 5 m 20 cm minus 2 m 60 cm?', 'options': ['2 m 60 cm', '3 m 60 cm', '2 m 40 cm', '3 m 40 cm'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! Borrow 1 m (100 cm): 120 cm - 60 cm = 60 cm. Metres: 4 - 2 = 2 m. Result = 2 m 60 cm.'}, {'question': 'A road is 10 km long. Workers have paved 6 km 400 m. How much road remains to be paved?', 'options': ['3 km 600 m', '4 km 600 m', '3 km 400 m', '4 km 400 m'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! 10 km 000 m - 6 km 400 m: Borrow 1 km (1,000 m): 1,000 - 400 = 600 m. km: 9 - 6 = 3 km. Result = 3 km 600 m.'}]}, {'id': 14, 'heading': 'Scaling Lengths', 'tagline': 'Multiplying and Dividing Lengths for Fabric and Road Laying', 'color': 'from-amber-600 to-orange-700', 'badge': 'Proportional Scaling', 'audio_text': 'When manufacturing clothes, building fences, or laying highway asphalt, we multiply and divide lengths to scale our quantities! Suppose a tailor needs 2 metres and 25 centimetres of fabric to stitch one school uniform shirt. How much fabric is required to stitch 4 identical shirts? We multiply the length by 4! Multiply the metres: 2 metres times 4 equals 8 metres. Multiply the centimetres: 25 centimetres times 4 equals 100 centimetres! Since 100 centimetres equals 1 metre, we add this 1 metre to our 8 metres, giving exactly 9 metres of fabric! What about division? Suppose a road construction crew paves 12 kilometres of highway equally over 4 days. How many kilometres do they pave each day? We divide: 12 kilometres divided by 4 equals 3 kilometres per day! Scaling lengths allows planners and builders to calculate materials, costs, and project timelines with absolute accuracy.', 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'Multiplying Mixed Units', 'content': 'Multiply metres and centimetres separately, then bundle every 100 cm into 1 m. (2 m 25 cm) × 4 = 8 m + 100 cm = 9 m.', 'icon': '✖️'}, {'title': 'Dividing Lengths', 'content': 'Divide total length by number of parts or days: 12 km / 4 = 3 km per day. 5 m / 2 = 2 m 50 cm per piece.', 'icon': '➗'}, {'title': 'Double Number Line for Cost', 'content': 'If 1 m of fabric costs ₹ 120, then 2 m costs ₹ 240, and 50 cm (0.5 m) costs ₹ 60. Proportional scaling connects length directly to currency.', 'icon': '🏷️'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'If 1 pair of trousers requires 1 m 50 cm of cloth, how much cloth is needed for 3 pairs?', 'options': ['4 m 50 cm', '3 m 50 cm', '4 m', '5 m'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! (1 m × 3) + (50 cm × 3) = 3 m + 150 cm = 3 m + 1 m 50 cm = 4 m 50 cm.'}, {'question': 'A 15-metre rope is cut into 5 equal pieces. How long is each piece?', 'options': ['3 metres', '5 metres', '2.5 metres', '3.5 metres'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! 15 m / 5 = 3 metres.'}]}, {'id': 15, 'heading': 'Height Estimation', 'tagline': 'Developing Real-World Spatial Benchmarks for Buildings and Trees', 'color': 'from-teal-600 to-cyan-700', 'badge': 'Estimation Skills', 'audio_text': "A skilled mathematician can estimate the height of a towering tree or building without carrying a giant measuring tape! How do we do this? By using familiar real-world benchmarks! Here are some reliable height benchmarks to keep in mind: The height of a standard single storey in a residential building or school is approximately 3 metres. Therefore, a three-storey school building is about 3 times 3, or approximately 9 to 10 metres tall! An adult person's height is usually between 1.6 and 1.8 metres. A standard classroom door is about 2 metres high. If you look at a mango tree outside your window that appears about twice as tall as the classroom door, you can estimate its height as roughly 4 metres! A streetlight pole is typically about 6 metres high. By comparing unfamiliar objects against these trusted mental anchors, you can make fast, reasonable estimations anytime, anywhere.", 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'Mental Benchmark Anchors', 'content': 'Classroom Door = ~2 m; Single Building Storey = ~3 m; Adult Person = ~1.7 m; Utility Light Pole = ~6 m; Ceiling Height = ~3 m.', 'icon': '🧠'}, {'title': 'Proportional Stacking', 'content': 'To estimate a tall tree: imagine how many classroom doors or people stacked head-to-toe would equal its height.', 'icon': '🌲'}, {'title': 'Estimation vs Guessing', 'content': 'Estimation is not random guessing; it is mathematical reasoning based on known real-world reference benchmarks.', 'icon': '🎯'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'A four-storey apartment building has floors of standard height (~3 m each). What is its estimated total height?', 'options': ['About 12 metres (4 × 3 m)', 'About 4 metres', 'About 40 metres', 'About 2 metres'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! 4 storeys × ~3 metres per storey ≈ 12 metres.'}, {'question': 'A flagpole is approximately three times the height of a 2-metre classroom door. What is its estimated height?', 'options': ['6 metres', '4 metres', '8 metres', '10 metres'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! 3 × 2 metres = 6 metres.'}]}, {'id': 16, 'heading': 'Feet and Inches', 'tagline': 'Everyday Imperial Units: Converting Inches, Feet, and Centimetres', 'color': 'from-purple-600 to-indigo-700', 'badge': 'Everyday Alternative Units', 'audio_text': "While the metric system is the universal international scientific standard, you will often hear people describe heights and screens in feet and inches! Our textbook explores these popular everyday units: An inch is approximately the length of the top joint of an adult's thumb, or about 2 centimetres and 5 millimetres (2.5 cm). There are exactly 12 inches in 1 foot! Since each inch is about 2.5 centimetres, 1 foot (12 inches) equals approximately 30 centimetres: the exact length of your long school ruler! When doctors and parents measure a child's height against a wall chart, a Grade 5 student might measure 4 feet 4 inches. Let us convert 4 feet into centimetres: 4 times 30 centimetres gives 120 centimetres! 4 inches is 4 times 2.5 centimetres, which is 10 centimetres. 120 plus 10 gives approximately 130 centimetres! Understanding both metric units and everyday feet and inches gives you complete mastery over human height and spatial dimensions.", 'theory_blocks': [{'title': 'The Imperial Conversions', 'content': '1 foot = 12 inches; 1 inch ≈ 2.5 cm (2 cm 5 mm); 1 foot ≈ 30 cm (standard 30 cm school ruler).', 'icon': '🦶'}, {'title': 'Height Measuring Chart', 'content': 'Grade 5 average height = ~4 ft 2 in to 4 ft 6 in (~125 cm to 138 cm). 5 feet = 5 × 30 cm = ~150 cm.', 'icon': '📏'}, {'title': 'Screen & Shoe Sizes', 'content': 'Television displays, mobile screens, and laptops are commonly measured in diagonal inches (e.g. 15.6-inch laptop screen).', 'icon': '📱'}], 'checkpoints': [{'question': 'How many inches are there in 1 foot?', 'options': ['12 inches', '10 inches', '100 inches', '24 inches'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Correct! Exactly 12 inches make up 1 foot.'}, {'question': 'Approximately how many centimetres are in 1 foot?', 'options': ['About 30 cm (the length of a long school ruler)', 'About 10 cm', 'About 100 cm', 'About 50 cm'], 'correct': 0, 'feedback': 'Spot on! 1 foot = 12 inches × 2.5 cm ≈ 30 cm.'}]}, {'id': 17, 'heading': 'Challenge 1', 'tagline': 'Mastery Quiz: Choosing Units, Equivalences, Comparisons, and Tallest Statues', 'color': 'from-indigo-600 to-purple-700', 'badge': 'Mastery Challenge • Part 1', 'audio_text': 'Welcome to Challenge 1! Test your foundational mastery of selecting sensible metric units, converting centimetres to metres, applying inequalities, and analyzing monument height data. Examine each question carefully, choose the correct answer, and review the detailed step-by-step solution modal for complete mathematical clarity.', 'quizzes': [{'question': 'Which unit is most appropriate to measure the depth of a village open water well?', 'options': ['Metres (m)', 'Centimetres (cm)', 'Kilometres (km)', 'Millimetres (mm)'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'A typical open well is 10 to 20 metres deep, making metres the most sensible and practical unit.'}, {'question': 'How many centimetres are there in 1 metre?', 'options': ['100 cm', '10 cm', '1,000 cm', '50 cm'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '1 metre is defined as exactly 100 centimetres (1 m = 100 cm).'}, {'question': 'How is 385 cm expressed in mixed metres and centimetres?', 'options': ['3 m 85 cm', '38 m 5 cm', '30 m 85 cm', '3 m 8 cm'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '385 cm = 300 cm + 85 cm = 3 m 85 cm.'}, {'question': 'Which symbol correctly compares: 6 m 25 cm ___ 650 cm?', 'options': ['< (Less than)', '> (Greater than)', '= (Equal to)', '≥ (Greater than or equal)'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '6 m 25 cm = 625 cm. Since 625 cm < 650 cm, 6 m 25 cm < 650 cm.'}, {'question': 'How tall is the Statue of Unity in Gujarat, India?', 'options': ['182 metres', '128 metres', '93 metres', '210 metres'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'The Statue of Unity stands at 182 metres, making it the tallest statue in the world.'}, {'question': 'The Statue of Liberty is 93 m and Spring Temple Buddha is 128 m. What is their height difference?', 'options': ['35 metres', '25 metres', '45 metres', '38 metres'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '128 m - 93 m = 35 metres.'}, {'question': 'A table cloth is 2 metres and 50 centimetres long. What is its length in centimetres?', 'options': ['250 cm', '205 cm', '2,500 cm', '25 cm'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '2 m = 200 cm. 200 cm + 50 cm = 250 cm.'}, {'question': 'Which of these lengths is the longest: 4 m, 380 cm, or 4 m 15 cm?', 'options': ['4 m 15 cm (415 cm)', '4 m (400 cm)', '380 cm', 'They are all equal'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Converting all to cm: 4 m = 400 cm; 380 cm; 4 m 15 cm = 415 cm. 415 cm is the longest.'}, {'question': 'Why is kilometres the preferred unit for inter-city highways instead of metres?', 'options': ['Highway distances are vast; writing 250 km is simpler than writing 250,000 m', 'Kilometres are cheaper to measure', 'Metres cannot be measured outdoors', 'Road signs only have space for two letters'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Using kilometres keeps numerical values manageable and easy to read on highway milestone signs.'}, {'question': 'A cotton stole is 2 metres long. How many 50-centimetre handkerchiefs can be cut from it?', 'options': ['4 handkerchiefs (200 / 50)', '2 handkerchiefs', '5 handkerchiefs', '10 handkerchiefs'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '2 metres = 200 cm. 200 cm / 50 cm = 4 handkerchiefs.'}]}, {'id': 18, 'heading': 'Challenge 2', 'tagline': 'Mastery Quiz: Measuring Instruments, Kilometres, Race Logistics, and Railway Records', 'color': 'from-teal-600 to-emerald-700', 'badge': 'Mastery Challenge • Part 2', 'audio_text': 'Welcome to Challenge 2! Test your understanding of outdoor measuring wheels, laser distance meters, kilometre conversions, race planning, and the Vivek Express rail route. Calculate each step methodically and inspect the solution modal whenever you need guidance.', 'quizzes': [{'question': 'What is a rolling wheel tool used by road surveyors to measure ground distances called?', 'options': ['A measuring wheel (rodometer)', 'A galvanometer', 'A protractor', 'A micrometer screw'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'A measuring wheel (rodometer) tracks revolutions as it rolls along the ground to measure distance.'}, {'question': 'How many metres are in 1 kilometre?', 'options': ['1,000 metres', '100 metres', '10 metres', '10,000 metres'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '1 kilometre equals 1,000 metres (1 km = 1,000 m).'}, {'question': 'In a 3-km race course, how many total metres must the participants run?', 'options': ['3,000 metres', '300 metres', '30,000 metres', '1,500 metres'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '3 km = 3 × 1,000 m = 3,000 metres.'}, {'question': 'If water stations are placed every 500 metres on a 3-km course, at which mark is Station 3 located?', 'options': ['1,500 metres (1.5 km)', '1,000 metres', '2,000 metres', '500 metres'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Station 1 = 500 m; Station 2 = 1,000 m; Station 3 = 1,500 m (1.5 km).'}, {'question': 'What is the total distance of the Vivek Express route from Dibrugarh to Kanniyakumari?', 'options': ['4,189 km', '3,500 km', '2,850 km', '5,200 km'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': "The Vivek Express covers 4,189 km across 9 Indian states, making it India's longest train route."}, {'question': 'How many metres are in half a kilometre (1/2 km)?', 'options': ['500 metres', '250 metres', '100 metres', '750 metres'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '1/2 of 1,000 metres = 500 metres.'}, {'question': 'A runner completes five 400-metre laps around an athletics track. How many kilometres did they run?', 'options': ['2 km (5 × 400 m = 2,000 m = 2 km)', '1 km', '3 km', '4 km'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '5 × 400 m = 2,000 m. Since 1,000 m = 1 km, 2,000 m = 2 km.'}, {'question': 'A laser distance meter measures a hall to be 45.5 metres. How many centimetres is this?', 'options': ['4,550 cm', '455 cm', '45,500 cm', '45 cm'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '45.5 m × 100 = 4,550 cm.'}, {'question': 'If a train travels 750 km on Day 1 and 825 km on Day 2, what is the combined distance?', 'options': ['1,575 km', '1,550 km', '1,675 km', '1,475 km'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '750 + 825 = 1,575 km.'}, {'question': 'How many metres are in 2 km 350 m?', 'options': ['2,350 metres', '235 metres', '23,500 metres', '2,035 metres'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '2 km = 2,000 m. 2,000 m + 350 m = 2,350 metres.'}]}, {'id': 19, 'heading': 'Challenge 3', 'tagline': 'Mastery Quiz: Millimetres, Sprout Tracking, Precision Lines, and Addition of Lengths', 'color': 'from-cyan-600 to-blue-700', 'badge': 'Mastery Challenge • Part 3', 'audio_text': 'Welcome to Challenge 3! Dive into millimetre precision, sprout growth tracking, exact line segment drafting, and mixed-unit column additions. Solve each problem with speed and precision, and check your reasoning with the solution modal.', 'quizzes': [{'question': 'How many millimetres are there in 1 centimetre?', 'options': ['10 mm', '100 mm', '1,000 mm', '5 mm'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Each centimetre contains 10 equal millimetres (1 cm = 10 mm).'}, {'question': 'A sprouted moong seed measured 5 mm on Day 1 and 12 mm on Day 2. What was its daily growth?', 'options': ['7 mm (12 - 5)', '17 mm', '6 mm', '8 mm'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Growth = 12 mm - 5 mm = 7 mm.'}, {'question': 'A line segment is 68 millimetres long. Express this in centimetres and millimetres.', 'options': ['6 cm 8 mm', '68 cm', '60 cm 8 mm', '6 cm 80 mm'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '68 mm = 60 mm + 8 mm = 6 cm 8 mm.'}, {'question': 'What is the sum of 3 m 45 cm and 2 m 75 cm?', 'options': ['6 m 20 cm', '5 m 20 cm', '6 m 15 cm', '5 m 120 cm'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'cm: 45 + 75 = 120 cm = 1 m 20 cm (write 20, carry 1 m). Metres: 3 + 2 + 1 = 6 m. Total = 6 m 20 cm.'}, {'question': 'Add 4 km 500 m and 3 km 800 m.', 'options': ['8 km 300 m', '7 km 300 m', '8 km 400 m', '7 km 1,300 m'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'm: 500 + 800 = 1,300 m = 1 km 300 m. km: 4 + 3 + 1 = 8 km. Total = 8 km 300 m.'}, {'question': 'How many millimetres are in 4 cm 5 mm?', 'options': ['45 mm', '405 mm', '54 mm', '450 mm'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '4 cm = 40 mm. 40 mm + 5 mm = 45 mm.'}, {'question': 'A pencil is 14 cm 2 mm long. Express its length in millimetres alone.', 'options': ['142 mm', '140 mm', '14.2 mm', '1,420 mm'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '14 cm = 140 mm. 140 mm + 2 mm = 142 mm.'}, {'question': 'Add 2 m 60 cm, 1 m 40 cm, and 3 m 25 cm.', 'options': ['7 m 25 cm', '6 m 25 cm', '7 m 15 cm', '6 m 125 cm'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'cm: 60 + 40 + 25 = 125 cm = 1 m 25 cm. Metres: 2 + 1 + 3 + 1 = 7 m. Total = 7 m 25 cm.'}, {'question': 'What is the thickness of a typical 5-rupee coin in millimetres?', 'options': ['About 2 mm', 'About 2 cm', 'About 20 mm', 'About 0.1 mm'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'A standard coin is roughly 2 millimetres thick.'}, {'question': 'On a double number line connecting cm and mm, what value aligns directly with 8 cm?', 'options': ['80 mm', '8 mm', '800 mm', '0.8 mm'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '8 cm × 10 = 80 mm.'}]}, {'id': 20, 'heading': 'Challenge 4', 'tagline': 'Mastery Quiz: Subtraction, Scaling, Height Estimation, and Imperial Units', 'color': 'from-emerald-600 to-teal-700', 'badge': 'Mastery Challenge • Part 4', 'audio_text': 'Welcome to the final challenge! Test your advanced mathematical mastery of length subtraction with borrowing, proportional fabric scaling, real-world estimation, and feet-inch conversions. Solve each puzzle, verify your score, and celebrate your mastery of Far and Near!', 'quizzes': [{'question': 'What is 8 m 20 cm minus 3 m 65 cm?', 'options': ['4 m 55 cm', '5 m 55 cm', '4 m 45 cm', '5 m 45 cm'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': 'Borrow 1 m (100 cm): 120 cm - 65 cm = 55 cm. Metres: 7 - 3 = 4 m. Result = 4 m 55 cm.'}, {'question': 'A 10-km marathon route has 4 km 350 m completed. How much distance remains?', 'options': ['5 km 650 m', '6 km 650 m', '5 km 750 m', '6 km 350 m'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '10 km 000 m - 4 km 350 m: Borrow 1 km (1,000 m): 1,000 - 350 = 650 m. km: 9 - 4 = 5 km. Result = 5 km 650 m.'}, {'question': 'If 1 shirt requires 2 m 25 cm of cloth, how much cloth is needed for 4 shirts?', 'options': ['9 metres (8 m + 100 cm = 9 m)', '8 m 50 cm', '8 m 25 cm', '10 metres'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '4 × 2 m = 8 m. 4 × 25 cm = 100 cm = 1 m. 8 m + 1 m = 9 metres.'}, {'question': 'A 20-metre rope is divided equally into 4 pieces. What is the length of each piece?', 'options': ['5 metres', '4 metres', '6 metres', '2.5 metres'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '20 m / 4 = 5 metres.'}, {'question': 'What is the estimated height of a 3-storey school building (standard floor height ~3 m)?', 'options': ['About 9 metres', 'About 3 metres', 'About 20 metres', 'About 30 metres'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '3 storeys × ~3 metres per storey ≈ 9 metres.'}, {'question': 'How many inches are there in 1 foot?', 'options': ['12 inches', '10 inches', '100 inches', '24 inches'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '1 foot is defined as exactly 12 inches.'}, {'question': 'Approximately how many centimetres are in 1 inch?', 'options': ['About 2.5 cm (2 cm 5 mm)', 'About 1 cm', 'About 5 cm', 'About 10 cm'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '1 inch is approximately 2.54 cm, commonly rounded to 2.5 cm (2 cm 5 mm).'}, {'question': 'A student measures 4 feet tall. Approximately what is their height in centimetres (1 foot ≈ 30 cm)?', 'options': ['About 120 cm (4 × 30 cm)', 'About 100 cm', 'About 140 cm', 'About 90 cm'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '4 feet × ~30 cm/foot ≈ 120 cm.'}, {'question': 'A classroom door is approximately 2 metres tall. A mango tree outside is twice as tall. What is its estimated height?', 'options': ['4 metres', '6 metres', '2 metres', '8 metres'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '2 × 2 metres = 4 metres.'}, {'question': 'If 1 metre of curtain fabric costs ₹ 150, what is the cost of 3 metres and 50 centimetres (3.5 m)?', 'options': ['₹ 525 (3 × 150 + 75)', '₹ 450', '₹ 500', '₹ 600'], 'correct': 0, 'solution_html': '3 m costs 3 × 150 = ₹ 450. 50 cm (half metre) costs ₹ 75. ₹ 450 + ₹ 75 = ₹ 525.'}]}]
